    "output": "# Code Review: `concurrency-1-b` (client 0)\n\n## Overview\n\nThe module consists of 108 nearly-identical functions `record_0` through `record_107`. Each function accepts a single `values` iterable, computes `sum(v * k for v in values)` where `k` is the function's index plus one, and returns a dict with keys `\"record\"` (the index), `\"total\"` (the weighted sum), and `\"count\"` (the length of `values`).\n\nThe code is truncated at `record_107`, which ends mid-expression (`\"total\":` with no value). That alone is a hard blocker.\n\nBelow is a detailed, numbered review.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Finding 1 \u2014 File is syntactically incomplete (blocker)\n\n`record_107` ends with:\n\n```python\ndef record_107(values):\n    total = sum(v * 108 for v in values)\n    return {\"record\": 107, \"total\":\n```\n\nThis is a `SyntaxError`. The module cannot be imported at all. Whatever produced this file (a generator, a copy/paste, an LLM, a truncation at a token limit) stopped mid-expression. Nothing else in this review matters until this is fixed.\n\n### Finding 2 \u2014 Massive, mechanical duplication (108 copies of one function)\n\nThere is exactly one logical operation here: \"weight the values by `(record_id + 1)` and return a summary dict.\" It is copy-pasted 108 times with the only differences being the literal `record` id and the multiplier. This violates DRY in the most extreme form.\n\nConsequences:\n\n- **Maintenance cost is 108\u00d7**: any change to the return shape, the summation, the validation, or the key names must be replicated in 108 places.\n- **Bug surface is 108\u00d7**: a single off-by-one in one copy is invisible without reading all of them. In fact the pattern here is `record_N` uses multiplier `N+1`, so `record_0 * 1`, `record_1 * 2`, \u2026 `record_107 * 108`. That is a 108-line opportunity for a typo, and there is no test proving the invariant holds across all of them.\n- **Review burden**: a reviewer must diff 108 near-identical bodies to confirm correctness. This is exactly the kind of file where a subtle wrong multiplier (e.g., `record_42` using `* 44`) would slip through.\n\n### Finding 3 \u2014 No single source of truth for the mapping `record_id -> multiplier`\n\nThe relationship \"record id `n` \u21d2 multiplier `n+1`\" is implicit and repeated 108 times. If the domain rule ever changes (e.g., multiplier becomes `n`, or `2*n`, or a lookup table), every function must change. There is no constant, table, or formula expressing the rule.\n\n### Finding 4 \u2014 No dispatcher / registry\n\nThere is no `record(values, id)` or `RECORDS` mapping. A caller who has a runtime `record_id` cannot call the right function without building a name like `f\"record_{id}\"` and doing `globals()[name]` \u2014 which is fragile, untyped, and will `KeyError`/`NameError` for out-of-range ids. There is no public API surface at all; the module is 108 free-floating functions.\n\n### Finding 5 \u2014 No input validation\n\n- `values` is assumed iterable of numbers. A string would iterate characters and raise `TypeError` deep inside `sum`; a generator would be consumed once (fine here since only one pass, but `len(values)` on a generator raises `TypeError`).\n- `len(values)` requires a sized container. A generator or iterator passed in will raise `TypeError: object of type 'generator' has no len()`. So despite the `values` name suggesting \"any iterable,\" only sized sequences work. This is an undocumented constraint.\n- Non-numeric values produce `TypeError`/`ValueError` from the arithmetic, with no context about which record failed.\n\n### Finding 6 \u2014 `len(values)` after `sum(...)` is a second traversal and a hidden constraint\n\n`sum(v * k for v in values)` consumes `values` once; `len(values)` then requires it to be sized. For a list this is fine (two O(1)/O(n) passes), but for any one-shot iterable it fails. If the intent is \"accept any iterable,\" the function should materialize once:\n\n```python\nvalues = list(values)\n```\n\nor count while summing. As written, the contract is silently \"sized sequence of numbers.\"\n\n### Finding 7 \u2014 `v * 1`, `v * 2`, \u2026 `v * 108` are hardcoded magic numbers\n\nEach",
